    "observations": [
      "Every business-application lookup in this case is keyed by sys_id. Two applications are named \"SAP HANA\" — 5470cc8583d31210bd2a5860ceaad372 (empty), 6e81683483431210bd2a5860ceaad398 (empty) — so a lookup by name reads a status belonging to the other record.",
      "The LWC computes its own roll-up in cafDashboard.build() from the mirrored CAF_Status__c values. It never reads u_caf_compliance_status, which is the field TC-SN-017 found wrong — so the two surfaces are answering from different data, not from one shared answer.",
      "Separate observation worth raising on its own: allGreen compares against controls.length, the count of columns across the whole grid rather than the count of controls that application actually has a request for. An application with fewer requests than there are columns can therefore never read Compliant, regardless of its own compliance."
    ],
    "expected": [
      "The Salesforce roll-up matches the ServiceNow widget exactly.",
      "An application reads Compliant only when every one of its controls is in_compliance."
    ],
    "actual": [
      "SAP Fiori — LWC \"1 of 6\"; ServiceNow stored status \"(empty)\"; 1 of 6 request(s) in compliance.",
      "SAP HANA — LWC \"1 of 6\"; ServiceNow stored status \"(empty)\"; 1 of 6 request(s) in compliance.",
      "Zscalar App — LWC \"Compliant\"; ServiceNow stored status \"(empty)\"; 6 of 6 request(s) in compliance."
    ],
    "verdictReason": "The two platforms visibly disagree about identical data — the split-brain outcome the case calls the worse of the two. Zscalar App: ServiceNow u_caf_compliance_status = \"(empty)\", Salesforce LWC renders \"Compliant\" (6 of 6 requests in compliance). The LWC is giving the arithmetically correct answer and ServiceNow the wrong one; the defect is in the ServiceNow business rule CAF - update BA when req compliant, not in the mirror.",
